[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Distributed Transactions & The Saga Pattern (Orchestration vs Choreography)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a monolithic architecture, database transactions are simple thanks to ACID properties and single-database management systems. However, moving to microservices means data is decentralized. Each microservice manages its own database. When a business process spans multiple services, maintaining data consistency without distributed locks (like 2PC) becomes a primary challenge. This is where the Saga Pattern comes in."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is the Saga Pattern?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Saga is a sequence of local transactions. Each local transaction updates data within a single service. If a step fails, the saga executes a series of compensating transactions that undo the changes made by the preceding steps. There are two main ways to implement the Saga pattern: Orchestration and Choreography."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Choreography-Based Saga"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In choreography, there is no central coordinator. Microservices publish and listen to events on a message broker (like RabbitMQ or Kafka) and decide independently what action to take next."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Decentralized, loosely coupled, no single point of failure at the orchestrator level."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Hard to track the workflow, risk of cyclic dependencies, difficult to debug as the system grows."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Orchestration-Based Saga"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In orchestration, a centralized component (the Orchestrator) tells each service what local transaction to execute and when. The orchestrator maintains the state of the entire workflow."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Centralized logic, easy to understand workflows, avoids cyclic dependencies."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: The orchestrator can become a single point of failure and a bottleneck if not designed carefully."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation Example (Orchestration)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is a conceptual Node.js code snippet showing an Orchestrator managing an Order Saga containing Order, Payment, and Inventory services."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"async function executeOrderSaga(orderData) {
  let orderId = null;
  let paymentId = null;

  try {
    // Step 1: Create Order
    orderId = await OrderService.createPending(orderData);

    // Step 2: Process Payment
    paymentId = await PaymentService.charge(orderData.amount);

    // Step 3: Update Inventory
    await InventoryService.reserveItems(orderData.items);

    // Complete Saga
    await OrderService.complete(orderId);
    return { status: "SUCCESS" };

  } catch (error) {
    // Compensating Transactions
    if (paymentId) {
      await PaymentService.refund(paymentId);
    }
    if (orderId) {
      await OrderService.cancel(orderId);
    }
    return { status: "FAILED", reason: error.message };
  }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing between orchestration and choreography depends on your team's familiarity with event-driven architectures and the complexity of your domain. For simple flows, choreography works well. For complex business processes with strict auditing requirements, orchestration is usually the safer, more maintainable choice."}]
